WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.865
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So far, we've talked

00:00:01.865 --> 00:00:04.590
all things global load balancing
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.965
STEPHANIE WONG:
But how does this

00:00:05.965 --> 00:00:08.550
work with Google Kubernetes
clusters running as backends?

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.550
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.514
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.640
Since last time, Beyond
Treats' online business

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.230
has still been booming.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.200
And, while they upgraded to
using Google's global load

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.510
balancer, they also
decided to move

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.963
to a microservices environment
for their web backend.

00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:33.380
STEPHANIE WONG:
But load balancers

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.570
were initially built to
support resource allocation

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.430
for virtual
machines, which helps

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.088
workloads be highly available.

00:00:39.088 --> 00:00:40.880
When containers and
container orchestrators

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.880
started to take off, users
adapted these VM-unfocused load

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.400
balancers for their use,
even though the performance

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.510
was suboptimal.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Traditionally, HTTPS load

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.440
balancers, targeting
Kubernetes clusters,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.660
would actually be
targeting its nodes

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.230
because they didn't have a
way to recognize each pod.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.660
In the absence of a way
to define a group of pods

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.930
as backends, the load
balancer used instance groups

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.590
to group VMs as a backend.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.200
Ingress support in GKE,
using those instance groups,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.740
used the HTTPS load balancer
to perform load balancing

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.182
to nodes in the cluster.

00:01:14.182 --> 00:01:15.890
STEPHANIE WONG: IP
table rules programmed

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.900
on the nodes raw
request to the pod

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.660
serving as backends for the
load balanced application.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.090
Load balancing to the
nodes was the only option

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.730
since the load balancer didn't
recognize pods or containers

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.400
as backends, resulting
in imbalanced load

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.820
and a suboptimal data path with
additional unnecessary hops

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:32.403
between nodes.

00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:33.820
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
But then Google

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.600
came out with Network
Endpoint Groups,

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.470
or NEGs, abstraction layer that
enables container native load

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.470
balancing.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.670
This means the load balancer
has visibility into a Kubernetes

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.400
cluster pods because
NEGs are integrated

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.180
with the Kubernetes ingress
controller running on GCP.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.190
In the case for
Beyond Treat, they

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.950
have a multi-tiered
e-commerce deployment

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.060
and want to expose one service
to the internet using GKE.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.730
With NEGs, they can
now provision and HTTPS

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.190
load balancer, allowing
them to configure

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.567
pad-based or host-based
routing to their backend pods.

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:09.650
STEPHANIE WONG: All they
have to do to get started

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.680
is to create a
VPC-native GKE cluster.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.900
By default, Kubernetes
uses static routes

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.600
for pod networking, which
requires a Kubernetes control

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.210
plane to maintain these
routes to each node.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.530
But this comes at
a scaling cost.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.860
In GKE, you have the
option to create clusters

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.560
in VPC-native mode,
which provides container

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.350
native load balancing
that uses a NEG model.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.330
VPC-native mode means
you have connectivity

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.630
between all pods in your VPC
without the overhead of route

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:36.710
scaling.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.780
And traffic is
evenly distributed

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.697
among the available healthy
backends in an input group.

00:02:41.697 --> 00:02:44.030
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now that
Beyond Treat has implemented

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.520
a VPC-native cluster
with NEGs, they've

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.860
experienced even
better load balancing.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.630
With container-native
load balancing,

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.930
traffic is distributed evenly
among the available healthy

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.500
backends in an endpoint group,
following their defined load

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.368
balancing algorithm.

00:02:58.368 --> 00:03:00.410
STEPHANIE WONG: They also
now have native support

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:05.510
for health checking including
TCP, HTTPS, HTTP/2 checks.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.760
NEGs can check the pods
directly rather than

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.850
the nodes forwarding health
checks to a random pod.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.310
As a result, health checks
more accurately mirror

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.645
the health of the backends.

00:03:14.645 --> 00:03:16.520
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Beyond
Treat also benefits

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.880
from graceful termination,
meaning, when a pod is removed,

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.430
the load balancer automatically
drains the connection

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.650
to the endpoint,
serving traffic based

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.252
on the connection draining
period configured for it.

00:03:27.252 --> 00:03:29.210
STEPHANIE WONG: And, best
yet, their pet lovers

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:32.720
will experience their traffic
hitting an optimal data path.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.270
With the ability to load
balance directly to containers,

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.580
the traffic hot from the
load balancer to the node

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.320
disappears since
load balancing is

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.420
performed in a single
step rather than two.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.045
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
And Beyond Treat now

00:03:43.045 --> 00:03:45.190
has increased
visibility and security.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.940
Container-native
load balancing helps

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.880
them troubleshoot their
services at the pod level.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.570
It can preserve the source IP
to make it easier to trace back

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.160
to the source of the traffic.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.830
Since the container sees the
packet arrive from the load

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:01.490
balancer rather than through a
source NEG from another node,

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:05.300
they can now create firewall
rules using node level network

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:05.800
policies.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.717
STEPHANIE WONG: It looks
like Beyond Treat has

00:04:07.717 --> 00:04:09.530
gone from the
popular, local bakery

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:12.320
to an international,
online bakery phenomenon.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.090
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
And its GCP backend

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.800
has grown along with it.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.970
We learned about the load
balancing options on GCP

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.170
and proved that
global load balancing

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:25.190
is more performant and reliable
than regional HTTPS load

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:25.940
balancing.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.732
STEPHANIE WONG: And,
finally, we showed you

00:04:27.732 --> 00:04:30.380
that, even with GKE clusters,
container-native load balancing

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.930
means they can target
back in pods directly

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.360
for optimal traffic
distribution and improvements

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.110
in scalability.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:36.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.880
All right, we've been talking
about trees for weeks,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.547
and Bell still hasn't
gotten to try any.

00:04:39.547 --> 00:04:42.980
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
we can get her to try some.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.260
That's all for today on
Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.690
Let us know how you're liking
this series in the comments

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:49.910
